<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Spanish Tripoli</h1><p class="st-meta">1510 CE – 1530 CE · capital Tripoli</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Tripoli under Spanish rule after its capture by Pedro Navarro in 1510, a brief Habsburg outpost later handed to the Knights of St John.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In July 1510, following his capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bejaia">Bejaia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-navarro">Pedro Navarro</a> stormed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-tripoli">Tripoli</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II of Aragon</a>. The town, then an autonomous city loosely tied to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate</a>, was sacked and much of its population fled or was killed.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Spanish <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-tripoli">Tripoli</a> was a small garrison administered through the viceroyalty of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily</a>. Costly to supply and isolated, it served mainly to deny the port to corsairs. Its fortifications were rebuilt, but the town recovered little of its former trade.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1530 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> granted <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-tripoli">Tripoli</a>, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a>, who had lost <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, creating <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hospitaller-tripoli">Hospitaller Tripoli</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/spanish-tripoli.jpg" alt="Spanish Tripoli" width="320" height="470"><figcaption>Image: Piri Reis · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tripoli_by_Piri_Reis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1510 CE – 1530 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tripoli</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ferdinand II of Aragon, Charles V</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/libya">Libya</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Habsburg Tripoli</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1520&amp;focus=spanish-tripoli" aria-label="Spanish Tripoli on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5018 983 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Spanish Tripoli lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5338 1239L4910 989M5338 1239L5338 1239"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4910 989h0M5338 1239h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5338 1239h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5338 1239h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tripoli. <a href="/atlas?year=1520&amp;focus=spanish-tripoli">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
